---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import data from '../../data/editorial-data.json';
import { editorialExample, editorialRoutes } from '../../data/editorial-examples';
import { editorialNames } from '../../lib/editorial';
import '../../styles/editorial.css';
const kinds = ['dumbbell', 'slopegraph', 'small-multiples', 'bar'] as const;
const schema = [
  {
    '@type': 'Article',
    headline: 'Life expectancy: four charts, different questions',
    datePublished: '2026-09-09',
    dateModified: '2026-09-09',
    author: { '@id': 'https://www.chartsai.com/#organization' },
  },
  {
    '@type': 'Dataset',
    name: 'Life expectancy in India, Brazil, Japan and the United States, 2000–2023',
    description:
      '96 annual World Bank WDI SP.DYN.LE00.IN observations, deliberately selected for a worked comparison. No averaging or rounding in the CSV.',
    license: 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/',
    creator: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'World Bank' },
    isBasedOn: 'https://data.worldbank.org/indicator/SP.DYN.LE00.IN',
    temporalCoverage: '2000/2023',
    distribution: {
      '@type': 'DataDownload',
      encodingFormat: 'text/csv',
      contentUrl: 'https://www.chartsai.com/editorial/data/life-expectancy.csv',
    },
  },
];
---

<Layout
  title="Life Expectancy Charts: Endpoints vs Trends | ChartsAI"
  description="Explore four original charts of World Bank life expectancy data. Compare endpoints with annual trends, inspect all values and download editable chart projects."
  schema={schema}
  imageHeight={900}
  imageAlt="Original source-based publication chart by ChartsAI"
  imagePath="/editorial/assets/dumbbell.png"
  dateModified="2026-09-09"
>
  <article class="container editorial-resource">
    <nav class="breadcrumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/editorial-charts/">Editorial charts</a><span>/</span><span>Life expectancy</span>
    </nav><h1>Four charts.<br />Different questions.</h1><p>
      A before-and-after chart can show an increase while hiding a reversal in between. This worked resource
      uses the same World Bank life expectancy data to compare four useful views: paired endpoints, a
      slopegraph, full annual paths and a one-year bar chart.
    </p><p>
      By ChartsAI / <a href="https://www.supamakers.com/">SupaMakers</a> · Reviewed September 9, 2026. Data: 2000–2023,
      retrieved September 9, 2026.
    </p>
    <h2>What is being measured?</h2><p>
      The <a href="https://data.worldbank.org/indicator/SP.DYN.LE00.IN">World Bank indicator SP.DYN.LE00.IN</a
      > reports life expectancy at birth in years, using UN Population Division and national sources. It is a period
      population estimate based on mortality patterns, not a prediction of an individual person’s lifespan. India,
      Brazil, Japan and the United States are a deliberate illustrative selection, not a representative sample of
      the world.
    </p>
    {
      kinds.map((kind, i) => (
        <section>
          <>
            <h2>
              {
                [
                  'Which endpoints changed?',
                  'How do the two periods compare?',
                  'What happened between the endpoints?',
                  'What were the 2023 levels?',
                ][i]
              }
            </h2>
            <figure class="editorial-figure">
              <>
                <img
                  src={`/editorial/assets/${kind}.svg`}
                  width="1200"
                  height={kind === 'small-multiples' ? 1200 : 900}
                  loading="lazy"
                  alt={editorialExample(kind).title}
                />
                <figcaption>
                  {
                    [
                      'The dumbbell retains each country’s two estimates and separates categories into rows. The width of a connector is an absolute difference in years.',
                      'The slopegraph places the same endpoint pairs on a shared vertical scale. Crossings describe changes in relative order, not the date those changes occurred.',
                      'Small multiples retain all 24 annual observations for each country. Every panel has the same value scale, so levels remain comparable. Intermediate reversals are visible here.',
                      'The bar chart selects 2023 only. Its zero-based axis compares levels, while omitting earlier change. It is a snapshot of this pinned period, not a claim to show the latest available year.',
                    ][i]
                  }
                </figcaption>
              </>
            </figure>
            <div class="editorial-links">
              <a class="button secondary small" href={editorialRoutes[kind]}>
                Edit this {editorialNames[kind].toLowerCase()}
              </a>
              {['svg', 'png', 'pdf', 'csv', 'json', 'html'].map((format) => (
                <a href={`/editorial/assets/${kind}.${format}`} download>
                  {format === 'json' ? 'Editable project' : format.toUpperCase()}
                </a>
              ))}
            </div>
          </>
        </section>
      ))
    }
    <h2>Calculate the difference without overstating it</h2><p>
      For India, the selected estimates are 62.749 years in 2000 and 72.003 in 2023: 72.003 − 62.749 = 9.254
      years. Brazil’s difference is 75.848 − 69.584 = 6.264 years. These are absolute differences in years,
      not percentage points. All four selected countries have higher 2023 endpoints; the full annual series is
      needed to assess the intervening path.
    </p><p>
      The source supplies estimates at different decimal precision. Chart labels round to one decimal for
      reading; downloads preserve the full supplied precision. Precision in the file should not be mistaken
      for certainty of the estimates. No causal effect, forecast or global average is calculated here.
    </p>
    <h2>Data, provenance and reuse</h2><p>
      We retained all 96 observations from the four-country, 2000–2023 API selection. The endpoint charts
      select the first and last year; the bar chart selects the last year; the panels retain every annual row.
      No missing values occurred in this selection and none were filled. Rows are in increasing year order.
    </p><p>
      World Bank data is offered under <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/">CC BY 4.0</a> on the
      indicator page. Credit World Bank WDI and its underlying sources, link the indicator and license, and identify
      your changes. Our charts and selection do not imply World Bank endorsement.
    </p><div class="editorial-links">
      <a href="/editorial/data/life-expectancy.csv" download>Full selected CSV</a><a
        href="/editorial/data/worldbank-life-expectancy-source.json"
        download>Original API response</a
      ><a href="/editorial/data/manifest.json" download>Selection and checksums</a>
    </div>
    <details open id="data">
      <summary>All 24 annual rows · unit: years</summary><div class="table-scroll">
        <table>
          <caption>Life expectancy at birth, years · World Bank WDI</caption><thead
            ><tr>{data.life[0].map((v) => <th scope="col">{v}</th>)}</tr></thead
          ><tbody
            >{
              data.life
                .slice(1)
                .map((row) => <tr>{row.map((v, i) => (i ? <td>{v}</td> : <th scope="row">{v}</th>))}</tr>)
            }</tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </details>
    <h2>Use the right chart for the claim</h2><p>
      Choose a <a href="/dumbbell-chart-maker/">dumbbell</a> when the gap is the focus, a <a
        href="/slopegraph-maker/">slopegraph</a
      > when the pair of periods is the organizing idea, or <a href="/small-multiples-chart-maker/"
        >small multiples</a
      > when the annual path matters. Read the <a href="/editorial-charts/publishing-guide/"
        >publishing guide</a
      > before embedding the result. This is one reusable worked resource, not four competing pages about the same
      dataset.
    </p>
  </article></Layout
>
